{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-14248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/cac89f66c8c1e5e0ac6a9857cb7db48d3ca6edc2a83fb2ba3a63b0809cfe6ab0.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-14248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 25 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =25-4 \\\\ y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-14248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c967ae346e33acb5a1fd1b128b82a503cd2a3cc735baebf5c5478bb4379d0463"} {"question" "q-14248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-14248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "19"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "23"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-14248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/0a9d728a5927f380bbc88445c6a409974d94050040f23000cce50f70de436917.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $-4$ and $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c967ae346e33acb5a1fd1b128b82a503cd2a3cc735baebf5c5478bb4379d0463"} {"question" "q-73831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-4$ and $-2$"] ["text" "$-5$ and $-1$"] ["text" "$-\\frac{13}{3}$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}$"] ["text" "$-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$"] ["text" "$-\\sqrt{6}-1$ and $-\\sqrt{6}+1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-4$ and $-2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/9a7f256ae77447f4b426612a3e136ccb60ebd172a6eb472c9fa9948e324b3085.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+3)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-6),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(-2+8) \\\\ 9 & =4p(6) \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =6-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c967ae346e33acb5a1fd1b128b82a503cd2a3cc735baebf5c5478bb4379d0463"} {"question" "q-73833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-73833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-79252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/aa95f5a4629b2f97943db55125f91d76de962534be820fe70112132e5fddaaec.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-79252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-79252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c967ae346e33acb5a1fd1b128b82a503cd2a3cc735baebf5c5478bb4379d0463"} {"question" "q-79252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-79252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$1$ and $-3$"] ["text" "$2$ and $-4$"] ["text" "$3$ and $-5$"] ["text" "$\\frac{3}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$"] ["text" "$\\frac{5}{2}$ and $-\\frac{7}{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-79252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$1$ and $-3$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-251796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/cec5eacc688d3c3a619aeb6ede2b1c9f9290b8045699259d4c1d933ecefd1835.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-251796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-4)^{2} & =4p(3+6) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+6) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+6) \\\\ 4 & =y+6 \\\\ y & =4-6 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-251796" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c967ae346e33acb5a1fd1b128b82a503cd2a3cc735baebf5c5478bb4379d0463"} {"question" "q-251796" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"} {"question" "q-251796" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/state.json" "file_sha256" "36316fc056f985b6e75e2e3482afc4fe25541e355bccd6d745b7c5f7767c6cdf"}] "usage" {"q-14248" []} "basis" 759 "retractions" [[17592186097039 :question/problem "![